.TH pdftotext 1 "15 August 2011"
.SH NAME
pdftotext \- Portable Document Format (PDF) to text converter
(version 3.03)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pdftotext
[options]
.RI PDF-file
.RI [ text-file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Pdftotext
converts Portable Document Format (PDF) files to plain text.
.PP
.B Deprecated in the DocSlurp fork:
use
.BR pdftomarkdown " FILE.pdf"
to extract text instead.
It runs pdftotext with the right options and converts the result to
Markdown with layout, tables and headings.
Direct use of pdftotext, and of its output formats, is not supported for
DocSlurp ingestion; pdftotext is kept as the backend pdftomarkdown calls.
See DOCSLURP.md in the source tree.
.PP
Pdftotext reads the PDF file,
.IR PDF-file ,
and writes a text file,
.IR text-file .
If
.I text-file
is not specified, pdftotext converts
.I file.pdf
to
.IR file.txt .
If
.I text-file
is \'-', the text is sent to stdout.  If
.I PDF-file
is \'-', it reads the PDF file from stdin.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " number"
Specifies the first page to convert.
.TP
.BI \-l " number"
Specifies the last page to convert.
.TP
.BI \-r " number"
Specifies the resolution, in DPI.  The default is 72 DPI.
.TP
.BI \-x " number"
Specifies the x-coordinate of the crop area top left corner
.TP
.BI \-y " number"
Specifies the y-coordinate of the crop area top left corner
.TP
.BI \-W " number"
Specifies the width of crop area in pixels (default is 0)
.TP
.BI \-H " number"
Specifies the height of crop area in pixels (default is 0)
.TP
.B \-layout
Maintain (as best as possible) the original physical layout of the
text.  The default is to \'undo' physical layout (columns,
hyphenation, etc.) and output the text in reading order.
.TP
.BI \-fixed " number"
Assume fixed-pitch (or tabular) text, with the specified character
width (in points).  This forces physical layout mode.
.TP
.B \-raw
Keep the text in content stream order.  This is a hack which often
"undoes" column formatting, etc.  Use of raw mode is no longer
recommended.
.TP
.BI \-remove-hyphens " mode"
Controls end-of-line hyphen handling.
\fImode\fR is one of:
.RS
.TP
.B all
Remove end-of-line hyphens (U+002D, U+2010, U+FE63, U+FF0D) and Unicode
soft hyphens (U+00AD), merging words across lines (default).
.TP
.B soft
Remove only Unicode soft hyphens (U+00AD), merging words across lines.
ASCII and other hyphen characters are preserved.
.TP
.B none
Keep all end-of-line hyphens and line breaks unchanged.
.RE
This option has no effect in \-raw or \-layout mode.
.TP
.B \-nodiag
Discard diagonal text (i.e., text that is not close to one of the
0, 90, 180, or 270 degree axes). This is useful for skipping
watermarks drawn on body text.
.TP
.B \-htmlmeta
Generate a simple HTML file, including the meta information.  This
simply wraps the text in <pre> and </pre> and prepends the meta
headers.
.TP
.B \-bbox
Generate an XHTML file containing bounding box information for each
word in the file.
The page width and height are given in the same coordinate space as the word
boxes: they are rotated by the page's /Rotate entry and scaled by \-r.
Word text is XML-escaped; ASCII control characters (which are not allowed in
XML 1.0, and include tabs and line breaks that can appear in ActualText) are
replaced by a space.
.TP
.B \-bbox-layout
Generate an XHTML file containing bounding box information for each
block, line, and word in the file.
Page size and text escaping are as for \-bbox.
.TP
.B \-tsv
Generate a TSV file containing the bounding box information for each
block, line, and word in the file.
Page size is as for \-bbox.
Every ASCII control character in the text column (in particular tab, carriage
return and line feed, which ActualText can contain) and the Unicode line
separators U+0085, U+2028 and U+2029 is replaced by a single space, so each
word is exactly one row of twelve tab-separated columns.
No quoting or backslash escaping is used.
.TP
.B \-wordmeta
Used together with \-bbox, \-bbox-layout or \-tsv: output additional metadata for
every word. Without this option the output is unchanged.
With \-bbox and \-bbox-layout each <word> element gets these extra attributes
after yMax:
.IR fontName ,
.IR fontSize ,
.IR rotation ,
.IR spaceAfter ,
.IR invisible ,
.I color
and
.IR baseline .
With \-tsv these are seven extra trailing columns after the text column, named
.IR font_name ,
.IR font_size ,
.IR rotation ,
.IR space_after ,
.IR invisible ,
.I color
and
.IR baseline ;
page, block and line rows have these columns empty.
The font name is that of the word's first character, as stored in the PDF
(including any subset prefix such as ABCDEF+).
The font size is in the same coordinate space as the bounding boxes.
.I rotation
is 0 to 3 (multiples of 90 degrees, as for the text direction).
.I spaceAfter
is 1 if a space follows the word on its line, otherwise 0.
.I invisible
is 1 for invisible text (text render mode 3), such as the hidden text layer of
OCRed documents, otherwise 0.
.I color
is the colour the word is painted with, as #rrggbb (the stroke colour for
stroke-only text render modes).
.I baseline
is the y coordinate of the baseline for horizontal text (rotation 0 and 2) or
the x coordinate for vertical text (rotation 1 and 3), in the same space as the
bounding boxes.
.TP
.B \-glyphs
Used together with \-bbox-layout and \-wordmeta: also output glyph-level records,
for reading typeset math. Without this option the output is unchanged, and a reader
that does not know the records can skip them (they are separate elements).
Each <page> starts with a font table: one
.B <font>
element per font used on the page with
.I id
(stable within the document),
.I name
(without subset prefix),
.IR type ,
.IR enc ,
.I emb
(embedded),
.I sym
(symbolic),
.I cid
(CID-keyed),
.I tu
(has a ToUnicode CMap) and
.I tuMiss
(codes used on the page that the ToUnicode CMap does not map).
Each <word> on a horizontal line is followed by one
.B <g>
element per glyph, in drawing order:
.I x
and
.I w
(left edge and advance),
.I b
and
.I z
(baseline and font size, only when they differ from the word's),
.I f
(font id),
.I c
(character code, the CID for CID fonts),
.I g
(glyph id of CID TrueType fonts),
.I u
(the glyph's text as hex code points),
.I s
(where the text came from: t ToUnicode, n glyph name, c code or built-in fallback,
a ActualText; an ActualText record stands for the whole span) and
.I n
(the glyph name, when it says more than the text).
Named glyphs that extraction drops as whitespace (such as TeX extension glyphs
with raw codes below 0x20) follow the font table as
.B <xg>
elements with the same attributes.
With \-rules, thin axis-aligned stencil image masks (Ghostscript draws TeX rules
this way) are output as rules with
.IR src="mask" .
A page has at most 100000 glyph records and the document 4000000, a page table at
most 1000 fonts. Glyph metadata is also limited to 64 document fonts, 256 KiB
of decoded ToUnicode data per font and 8 MiB per document, 4096 mappings per
font and 65536 per document, and a 128 MiB metadata reservation budget.
External usecmap references are not followed for glyph metadata. Exceeding a
metadata budget stops further metadata collection. Past a cap the page gets
.I glyphsTruncated="1"
or
.IR fontsTruncated="1" .
.TP
.B \-images
With \-bbox-layout: also output where each visible image and image mask lands, as
.B <image>
elements (after the rules, if any) with
.IR xMin ,
.IR yMin ,
.IR xMax ,
.I yMax
(the part inside the clip and the page, in the words' coordinates),
.I width
and
.I height
(the image's own size in samples) and
.I kind="mask"
for an image mask,
.I ref="num gen"
for an image XObject (its indirect object reference, so repeats of one image can be
found; absent for inline images), then what the image dictionary says:
.I filter
(the codec: dct, jpx, jbig2, ccitt, flate, lzw, rl, none or other),
.I bpc
(bits per component, 0 when not given),
.I cs
(gray, rgb, cmyk, indexed, lab, icc1, icc3, icc4, sep, devn, other or none),
.I smask="1"
with a /SMask or /Mask,
.I decode="1"
with /Decode,
.I inline="1"
for an inline image, and
.I copy
(jpeg, bilevel, decode or render: how its pixels can be taken out without rendering, see
docslurp/page-facts/README.md). The PDF page number is
.I number
on
.BR <page> ,
and a page that is a scan gets
.I scan
(single, tiled or layered) and
.I scanImage
(the 1-based position of the covering image), last: its images cover at least 85% of it
(\-scan\-min\-coverage) and at most 20 words are visible (\-scan\-max\-words).
Only image dictionaries are read: image data is never decoded. Images under 2 points
are dropped, a page keeps at most 2000 (past that it gets
.IR imagesTruncated="1" )
and a document 100000.
.IP
It also summarises the page's painted vector drawing that is not axis-aligned (curves and
diagonal segments: the strokes of charts and diagrams), as
.B <graphic>
elements after the images with
.IR xMin ,
.IR yMin ,
.IR xMax ,
.I yMax
(the union of the paths' extents inside the clip and the page),
.I paths
(painted paths),
.I segments
(diagonal straight segments plus curve segments),
.I curves
(curve segments) and
.I directions
(in how many of 8 sectors of 180 degrees the diagonal segments point, 0 to 8). Paths made only
of axis-aligned straight segments (rules, boxes, table grids, underlines, frames) are not
counted; pattern fills, shadings and hidden optional content are not seen. Each path is
added to one cell of a fixed 16 by 16 grid over the page, so memory and time per path are
constant; the cells are merged where their boxes come within 3 points, giving at most 256
clusters a page. A document writes at most 100000 (past that a page gets
.IR graphicsTruncated="1" ).
.TP
.BI \-page\-facts\-fd " fd"
With \-bbox-layout: also write page facts (JSON lines, schema doppler-page-facts/1: a doc
line, one line per page, a run line; see pdfpagefacts(1) and docslurp/page-facts/README.md)
to the open descriptor
.I fd
(3 or more, open for writing, never standard output). The lines are byte-identical to
.BR pdfpagefacts 's
for the same range and level, and the text output does not change. Poppler's messages
are also counted per page (by category) while facts are written.
.TP
.BI \-page\-facts\-level " basic|full"
Page facts level (default basic).
.TP
.BI \-page\-facts\-include " list"
Page facts families added to basic: fonts, oddtext, images-detail, vectors, forms, annots,
ocg, structure (comma-separated).
.TP
.B \-page\-facts\-timing
Add factsMs to page lines, totalMs and peakRssKiB to the run line (not deterministic).
.TP
.BI \-scan\-max\-words " number"
A page with more visible words is not a scan, for page facts and \-images (default 20).
.TP
.BI \-scan\-min\-coverage " share"
The share of the page one image, or the images together, must cover for a scan, in (0,1]
(default 0.85).
.TP
.BI \-scan\-layer\-overlap " share"
The share of the page a second image overlapping the scan must cover to make it layered, in
(0,1] (default 0.5).
.IP
A share is a plain decimal number (0.8, .8, 1); anything else is a usage error. When any of
the three \-scan options is not its default, the page facts doc line records all three
.RI ( scanThresholds );
with the defaults the output does not change.
.TP
.B \-cropbox
Use the crop box rather than the media box with \-bbox, \-bbox-layout and \-tsv.
.TP
.BI \-colspacing " number"
Specifies how much spacing we allow after a word before considering adjacent text to be a new column, measured as a fraction of the font size. Current default is 0.7, old releases had a 0.3 default.
.TP
.BI \-enc " encoding-name"
Sets the encoding to use for text output. This defaults to "UTF-8".
.TP
.B \-listenc
Lists the available encodings
.TP
.BI \-eol " unix | dos | mac"
Sets the end-of-line convention to use for text output.
.TP
.B \-nopgbrk
Don't insert page breaks (form feed characters) between pages.
.TP
.B \-urls
Print the URL of each link next to its link text, enclosed in square
brackets at the end of the line (or line fragment) that contains the
linked text.  Links that do not point to a URL, such as links to another
place in the same document, are ignored.
Not compatible with \-bbox, \-bbox\-layout, \-tsv or \-htmlmeta.
.TP
.BI \-opw " password"
Specify the owner password for the PDF file.  Providing this will
bypass all security restrictions.
.TP
.BI \-upw " password"
Specify the user password for the PDF file.
.TP
.B \-q
Don't print any messages or errors.
.TP
.B \-v
Print copyright and version information.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage information.
.RB ( \-help
and
.B \-\-help
are equivalent.)
.SH BUGS
Some PDF files contain fonts whose encodings have been mangled beyond
recognition.  There is no way (short of OCR) to extract text from
these files.
.SH EXIT CODES
The Xpdf tools use the following exit codes:
.TP
0
No error.
.TP
1
Error opening a PDF file.
.TP
2
Error opening an output file.
.TP
3
Error related to PDF permissions.
.TP
4
The text output is complete, but the page facts (\-page\-facts\-fd) could not be written
(for example, the reader closed the pipe; SIGPIPE does not stop the run).
.TP
99
Other error.
